Tecmo World Cup '98 (Sega ST-V, stv.cpp) at the arcade bar: it starts with the ROM sets that
exist, and runs a match inside a frame on WebAssembly.

- stv.cpp: twcup98's default BIOS is the USA one (EPR-17952A). MAME 0.289's default ST-V BIOS,
  EPR-23603 (Japan 00/10/24), is a newer dump than the game sets that carry their BIOS files, so
  MAME refused to start; the game is English with the USA BIOS (MAME testers report the game's
  bugs the same with -bios us).
- segabill.cpp: the Sega billboard (the score display above a Versus City cabinet, in every
  ST-V machine) has its program ROM optional, zeros when missing; the sets don't carry its
  device set (segabill.zip). Without a program its Z80 is stopped after the reset (it ran zeros,
  ~2% of a frame), and nothing the game does depends on it.
- SH-2 interpreter (sh2.cpp, sh.cpp): idle loops are skipped. The driver's pcflush addresses
  (the recompiler's speedup hints, which MAME dropped when the recompiler is off: now kept) mark
  where a game waits: Tecmo World Cup '98's master calls an empty hook and reads a RAM flag that
  its vblank handler sets, its slave reads the FRT status until the master's MINIT sets the input
  capture flag; ~60% of both CPUs' instructions. At such an address a pass of the loop runs as
  usual; if it wrote no memory, took no interrupt and left every register as it was, every later
  pass in the same timeslice is the same (nothing else runs meanwhile), so all but the last are
  skipped with their cycles counted. The machine's state is the one the full loop gives:
  verified byte-identical against the plain interpreter over a 3600-frame two-player match (whole
  save state but the saved hint list, and every picture). Also, the interpreter fetches through
  the opcode space's cache, as the recompiler does (the same words, without the dispatch).
- VDP2 (saturn_v.cpp): caches for one screen update, where registers and VRAM are constant:
  each window's coordinates for the last line asked, each line's window test as two intervals
  (vdp2_window_process and the RBG windows did both windows' coordinates for every pixel), the
  colour offsets as a table per channel; the rotation layer's copy keeps the blending and offsets
  out of its pixel loop. Pictures byte-identical to before.

- Controls (libretro.cpp, patches/0002's per-game names): the cabinet's own A, B, C on Button
  1-3; a name "" leaves a button off the descriptors, and Button 4, which the game doesn't use,
  has it.

Measured in Chrome 154 (M1 Max, a match, 704x448): 25.1 -> 12.4 ms per frame (p50), budget 16.73.

